## useContext Problems and Solutions

## The Re-render Problem

```tsx
const AppContext = createContext({ user: null, theme: 'light', count: 0 });

function Header() {
  const { theme } = useContext(AppContext);
  // Re-renders whenever user OR count changes — even though Header only uses theme
  return <header className={theme}>...</header>;
}
```

## Solution 1: Split Contexts

```tsx
const ThemeContext  = createContext<'light' | 'dark'>('light');
const UserContext   = createContext<User | null>(null);
const CounterContext = createContext(0);

// Header only subscribes to ThemeContext — immune to user/count changes
function Header() {
  const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);
}
```

## Solution 2: Memoize Context Value

```tsx
function Provider({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState<User | null>(null);
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState<'light' | 'dark'>('light');

  // Without useMemo: new object every render → all consumers re-render
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, theme, setUser, setTheme }), [user, theme]);

  return <AppContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</AppContext.Provider>;
}
```

## Solution 3: External Store

For state that changes frequently (animation values, mouse position, form fields), replace context with Zustand or Jotai:

```tsx
const useStore = create((set) => ({
  count: 0,
  inc: () => set(s => ({ count: s.count + 1 })),
}));

// Only re-renders when count changes — not the whole store
const count = useStore(s => s.count);
```

## Context Is Not a State Manager

Context is a dependency injection mechanism, not a performance-optimised state solution. Use it for stable, low-frequency values (theme, locale, auth). Use a dedicated store for everything that updates regularly.

