## Error Boundaries in Functional Components

## The Gap

`componentDidCatch` and `getDerivedStateFromError` have no hook equivalents. You must use a class component as the boundary itself. However, you can keep the class boundary minimal and drive everything else from hooks.

## Minimal Class Boundary

```tsx
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component<
  { fallback: React.ReactNode; onError?: (e: Error) => void; children: React.ReactNode },
  { error: Error | null }
> {
  state = { error: null };

  static getDerivedStateFromError(error: Error) {
    return { error };
  }

  componentDidCatch(error: Error, info: React.ErrorInfo) {
    this.props.onError?.(error);
    logToSentry(error, info);
  }

  render() {
    return this.state.error ? this.props.fallback : this.props.children;
  }
}
```

## react-error-boundary (Recommended)

```tsx
import { ErrorBoundary, useErrorBoundary } from 'react-error-boundary';

// Declarative usage
function App() {
  return (
    <ErrorBoundary
      fallbackRender={({ error, resetErrorBoundary }) => (
        <div>
          <p>{error.message}</p>
          <button onClick={resetErrorBoundary}>Retry</button>
        </div>
      )}
      onError={(error, info) => logError(error, info)}
    >
      <Page />
    </ErrorBoundary>
  );
}

// Throw into nearest boundary from a functional component
function DataLoader() {
  const { showBoundary } = useErrorBoundary();

  useEffect(() => {
    fetchData()
      .catch(err => showBoundary(err)); // bubble to ErrorBoundary
  }, []);
}
```

## Note on Async Errors

Error boundaries only catch **render-time** errors. Async errors (in `useEffect`, event handlers) must be caught manually and either handled locally or thrown into the boundary via `showBoundary`.

